<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Potsdam Declaration</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam">Potsdam</a> Declaration was the statement issued on 26 July 1945 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> setting out the terms for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s surrender in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. It demanded the unconditional surrender of the armed forces, disarmament, occupation and the removal of militarist leaders, threatening 'prompt and utter destruction' otherwise. Japan accepted it on 14 August 1945, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atomic-bombings-of-hiroshima-and-nagasaki">atomic bombings</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-declaration-of-war-on-japan">Soviet declaration of war</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/potsdam-declaration.jpg" alt="Potsdam Declaration" width="320" height="395" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author or not provided · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:President_Harry_S._Truman_and_Prime_Minister_Winston_Churchill_on_the_steps_of_the_%22Little_White_House,%22_the..._-_NARA_-_198764.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
